WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.491
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:14.730
I'm Leilani.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:17.640
I'm a fifth year PhD
student here in CSAIL.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.370
I work on explanatory
artificial intelligence.

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:22.320
I'm interested in
processes and methods

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.670
to make sure that machines
can explain themselves,

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:27.420
especially when they
get into errors.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:31.500
My long term research is that
all machines, all processes,

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.600
even opaque processes like
machine learning systems,

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.570
will have the ability
to explain themselves;

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.730
they can be challenged in
an adversarial proceeding

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:40.800
if they do something
wrong; and also they'll

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.870
be able to communicate
with people,

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:45.180
so when we have humans
and machines working

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.550
on tasks together, they'll
be able to communicate

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:50.310
a common language.

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:53.370
I'm at the last stages
of my PhD research,

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:56.220
and I'm looking to evaluate
the methods I've been building

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:57.820
for the last couple of years.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:03.030
So we hired on a UROP to do
some simulations for the project

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:06.660
and then work on incorporating
my methods into the simulation

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.510
to prove that it's
accurate, to prove that it

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:11.660
works in this environment.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:15.150
So you have a vision system
that basically sees that.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:21.810
Then you have some car
that's at some angle here.

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:23.680
And if it's at
enough of an angle,

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:30.840
you basically want the brakes
engaged as well as the gas.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.990
So we want them to, basically,
have some sort of conversation

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:36.480
back and forth very quickly,
because they're never supposed

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:37.683
to be on at the same time.

00:01:37.683 --> 00:01:39.600
If you were going to
have a rule-based system,

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.120
it would say, OK this
is absolutely wrong.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.600
But if you have this
conversation between parts,

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.810
and maybe even a planner,
then it should come up with,

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:51.310
yeah, you have to
hold both of them.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:54.360
And then you can proceed
forward once this turns green.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.520
Once we pick the
simulation, I can see how

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.650
verbose my explanation will be.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:02.100
Because you don't want something
that's too long and too hard

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.470
to process if you're making
this real time decision.

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:06.334
Can a week be good for you?

00:02:06.334 --> 00:02:07.195
Eleven to--

00:02:07.195 --> 00:02:09.130
--twelve on Wednesdays?

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:09.630
Yeah.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:10.639
Yeah, works for me.

00:02:10.639 --> 00:02:12.025
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.070
My name is Divya
Shanmugam, and I

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:18.750
am a PhD student in the Clinical
and Applied Machine Learning

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:19.250
Group.

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:20.717
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.520
The lab that I'm in broadly
focuses on machine learning

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.950
and health care, but
what I'm excited about

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:45.030
is machine learning
on unreliable data.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:48.450
What is the best you can
do with data sets that

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.250
are maybe not labeled
correctly, or have

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.775
noise in their features,
or that kind of thing?

00:02:52.775 --> 00:02:54.400
Those are the problems
that I focus on.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:55.894
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:20.440
I'm sort of a night
person, and so the mornings

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:23.320
are a lot of paper
reading and slower tasks,

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:27.520
and then the afternoon is filled
with meetings or more coding.

00:03:31.272 --> 00:03:32.980
There's an organization
in the department

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:36.070
for the graduate
women in Course 6.

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:37.960
It's called GW6, and
one of the events

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:40.690
that it focused on
running this year

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:44.620
was a summit to celebrate
the women in academia

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:48.520
in the department and
connect people across years.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:51.190
I worked with a set
of women across years

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:53.050
to put it together.

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:56.320
So we're doing a walk through of
the building before the event,

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:58.720
and we're just
trying to make sure

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:00.670
that everything we think
is going to be there

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:05.510
is there and just previewing
what the event might look like.

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:07.007
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:33.300
Hi my name is Harini, and my
research focuses on the ways

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:37.080
that machine learning
tools interact with people.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.540
This can mean
anything from the ways

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:42.870
that people are involved in
the data collection process

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:46.320
and how that can lead
to negative consequences

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.430
to the ways that we can
build machine learning tools

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.080
to better serve end users.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:53.394
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:09.050
Natalie is another PhD
student in computer science.

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:11.210
Natalie and I have
actually worked together

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.730
on several projects,
and it's really great

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:16.040
to have someone to
collaborate with.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:18.950
Some of the things that we work
on are related to our research

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:22.610
and others are related
to side projects

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:24.230
that we're both interested in.

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:26.900
Something that we've both been
working on over the past year

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:30.380
is this YouTube series
called ML Tidbits.

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:31.880
--what exactly
people mean when they

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:34.640
talk about false positives,
or false negatives,

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:36.350
or true positives,
or true negatives.

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:39.950
The goal is to teach
machine learning concepts

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:43.310
in a really
accessible way that's

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:45.245
understandable to
a broad audience.

00:05:45.245 --> 00:05:46.564
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:55.900
There are a lot of talks
that happen around CSAIL.

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:58.390
It's really nice to be
able to go to these,

00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:01.360
because they're often from
areas that you don't necessarily

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:04.900
encounter in your own
everyday research.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:08.200
It's a nice way to break
up the normal stuff that we

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:10.870
do every day and learn
about research that's

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:13.810
happening, both from
students here at MIT,

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:18.190
from students elsewhere who are
visiting, from other faculty

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:20.260
in other departments.

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:23.560
The talk that we went
to on data and feminism

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:26.020
was by Catherine
D'Ignazio, who's

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:29.830
a new professor in the
urban planning department.

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:32.140
Her research is
also really related

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:34.070
to computer science
and data science.

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:37.990
So that was really interesting
for me to see the ways

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:40.540
that computer science and
the things that I'm doing

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:44.320
might apply to other fields
and other applications.

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:47.380
--intellectual history
that we inherit today

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:51.098
and that we can draw from and
use in our work going forward.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:57.010
There are a lot of fun social
gatherings here in CSAIL.

00:06:57.010 --> 00:07:00.840
One of the favorite things
that we do is Muffin Monday.

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:03.700
At 5:00 PM on Mondays, we
go up to the top floor,

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:06.850
and basically any
CSAIL graduate student

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:10.360
can have a free baked good,
and chat with friends,

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:12.310
and things like that.

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:13.810
Muffin Monday is awesome.

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:18.190
People come from all around
Stata to get these muffins.

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:21.430
Free food events are sort of
a staple of graduate student

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:22.660
culture in the department.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:25.600
It's a good place to spend
10 or 15 minutes catching up

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:28.070
with people who aren't
necessarily on your floor

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:30.640
or in your research area, and
the pastries are pretty good.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:32.107
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:40.450
In our group, we have
group meeting every week.

00:07:40.450 --> 00:07:43.390
We order lunch, and
usually one or two people

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:45.850
talk about something
they've been working on

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:49.030
or give a talk if they're
trying to practice a talk.

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:52.330
Sometimes people talk
about preliminary ideas

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:54.910
or go through things
on the whiteboard.

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:59.320
It's basically an opportunity
for people to bounce ideas off

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:02.830
of others in the group and get
feedback on things that they're

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:04.390
working on.

00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:06.550
It's nice to get
perspective on the thing

00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:09.700
that you're working on
from a range of people

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:13.610
with different backgrounds
and different expertises.

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:15.550
So let's start with
this project, which

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:17.380
is about data
augmentation to help

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:20.390
with one-shot medical
image segmentation.

00:08:20.390 --> 00:08:23.260
Now, in this particular
project, the medical images

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:25.645
that we're looking at are
magnetic resonance images,

00:08:25.645 --> 00:08:26.740
or MRIs.

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:30.114
If you've never seen one before,
this is what they look like.

00:08:30.114 --> 00:08:31.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:56.200
Nowadays, there's no in
vivo objective biomarker

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:57.210
for diagnosis.

00:08:57.210 --> 00:08:59.990
Well, photons can
actually provide

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:02.510
an alternative architecture
for quantum computing.

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:05.500
If you have a lot of data,
extracting useful information

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:07.840
will take a lot of human hours.

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:10.280
Silicon photonics
is CMOS-compatible,

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:12.550
so we don't have to
reinvent the wheel.

00:09:12.550 --> 00:09:16.910
Automation, sensors everywhere,
no people on the floor.

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:19.720
So if something breaks, we need
to find out really quickly.

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:22.360
This takes the output of
some opaque mechanism, which

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:25.150
could be anything from
the vision system of a car

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:27.130
or possibly a
planner of a vehicle

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:30.160
that may be proprietary
because the company wants

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:31.690
to keep that secret.

00:09:31.690 --> 00:09:35.360
Then this reasonableness
monitor provides an output

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:38.020
of both a judgment, so it says
whether the intended behavior

00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:41.740
is reasonable or not, and also
provides an explanation of why.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:43.219
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:57.000
Obviously being at MIT
and in CSAIL there's

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:57.880
a lot of pressure.

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:00.360
But I've been able to make
a lot of great friends,

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:02.280
and what's nice about
being a graduate student

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:04.770
is you have a very
flexible schedule.

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:06.630
I really love coffee,
so I try and take

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:10.050
a coffee break every few
days and go out with friends.

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:12.540
So Harini, and Divya,
and I will occasionally

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:15.720
go and get coffee and
just chat about things.

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:18.960
It's a nice excuse
to get out of lab

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:21.900
and to not only update
your friends about research

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:23.910
but also your life
surrounding that research

00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:26.370
and maintain that
support network,

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:30.180
because I think graduate school
can be isolating sometimes.

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:32.940
Having those moments where
you're connecting with friends

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:33.750
can be really nice.

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:40.820
I also find CSAIL to
be very collaborative.

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:43.640
At other universities I
been or in other workplaces,

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:45.530
I would work mainly
in isolation.

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:48.590
But what's nice about being
here is I can just turn my head

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:50.330
and ask my colleague a question.

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:53.930
People are always willing to
help, and chat about research,

00:10:53.930 --> 00:10:56.000
and collaborate.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:58.490
It's just a very nice
welcoming environment.

00:11:01.190 --> 00:11:03.340
